  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 141 PM EDT Sun Sep 13 2026\n\nDespite the NBM having no PoPs today, the HREF is in good agreement \nthat widely scattered showers will occur in northwest Indiana and \nsouthwest Lower Michigan from 20Z-00Z tonight as an upper level \ntrough and attendant 500mb shortwave that passes through the Great \nLakes. Rainfall amounts should be light, less than 0.10\" for most \nlocations. Surface observations show that the surface cold front \nassociated with this upper level system already passed through the \narea this morning. Incoming high pressure behind the front means the \npressure gradient will increase over the area this afternoon and \nevening, with wind gusts up to 20-25 mph Sunday afternoon and \nevening. Strong northwest flow and CAA behind the front will make \nfor a pleasant night tonight with lows in the upper 40s to low 50s. \nA very fall-like day is ahead Monday with highs only in the low to \nmid 70s, comfortable humidity, and some filtered sunshine as high \npressure will be directly overhead.\n\nIf you have plans to head to SE Lake Michigan beaches today, stay \nout of the water! There is a High Swim Risk and a Beach Hazards \nStatement is in effect until 03Z Monday. Behind the cold frontal \npassage this morning, dangerous swimming conditions have developed \nand are expected to persist through tonight for southeastern Lake \nMichigan beaches in Berrien and La Porte counties. Buoy observations \noffshore of Washington Park Beach and Silver Beach show waves of 3-4 \nft as of early afternoon. Moderate to strong rip and structural \ncurrents are expected. Hazardous swimming conditions may occur again \non Tuesday.\n\nAn upper level ridge will build across the central and eastern CONUS \nover the next several days. With WAA and southwesterly moisture \ntransport from the Gulf, heat and humidity will build on Tuesday \nunderneath the peak of the upper level ridge over the Great Lakes. \nOur far southwest will be the warmest with temperatures in the low \n90s for highs Tuesday and peak heat indices approaching Heat \nAdvisory criteria at around 97-98 degrees. Confidence is low \nfor peak heat indices due to chances for rain/storms (see below)\nand clouds, so will hold off on any heat headlines for now. \n\nAnother cold front approaches late Tuesday and Wednesday with zonal \nflow aloft as the ridge gets suppressed. Have maintained likely\nPoPs of 40-70% Tuesday afternoon through Wednesday. There is a \nMarginal Risk for severe weather on Tuesday; isolated damaging \nwind gusts will be possible due to up to 2000 J/kg of SBCAPE and\nsteepening low level lapse rates. With forcing expected to be \nweak, this could be a limiting factor for strong to severe \nstorms. There are still some uncertainties with this system in \nregards to exact timing and placement of the cold front. \nRain/storm chances midweek will be highly dependent on where the\nperiphery of the ridge ends up (which model guidance has \nroutinely struggled with this same setup in recent months). The \nboundary will likely stall somewhere across the Great Lakes or \nOhio River Valley as the upper level ridge shifts eastward, so \nhave maintained broad PoPs midweek of 30-50%.\n\nGiven that the overall synoptic pattern this fall will be influenced \nby a strong to potentially historic El Nino, guidance is slowly \ntrending towards near to slightly warmer than normal temperatures \nwith drier than normal conditions for mid to late September.",
